{% set local_deep_dive %}
<p>Here's the deal with Huntsville's drainage: the geography is working against you from multiple angles.</p>

<p>Start with location. Huntsville sits in the Tennessee River valley, which sounds scenic until you realize what that means for your property. The valley floor has a naturally higher water table than the surrounding highlands. When Wheeler Lake and the Tennessee River system are running high — like they do every spring — that water table rises even more. If you're in south Huntsville, Big Cove, or anywhere near the flood plain, the ground under your house is holding more moisture than you'd probably like to know about.</p>

<p>Now add our soil. North Alabama's red clay — that Ultisol that stains everything it touches — doesn't absorb water like regular dirt. Those fine, plate-like particles shed water sideways instead of letting it soak through. When rain hits clay, it pools. It spreads. And it finds the lowest point it can, which is often right against your foundation.</p>

<p>The expand-contract cycle is what really does the damage. When clay gets saturated, it swells — pushing against foundation walls with surprising force. We've seen basement walls bowed inward 1.5 inches from clay pressure alone. When it dries out, the clay shrinks and cracks — leaving gaps that the next rain exploits. Your foundation is caught in this constant tug-of-war. Those hairline cracks in your basement walls? That's the clay talking.</p>

<p>Then there's the growth factor. Huntsville is adding 16+ new residents every single day. The construction boom in Hampton Cove, Providence, and out toward Meridianville means thousands of acres of former farmland getting scraped, compacted, and built on. That compacted clay drains even worse than natural soil. And those new homes? They come with builder-grade gutters that meet code and nothing more — 5-inch gutters when 6-inch would be smarter, .027 gauge aluminum when .032 lasts twice as long. Gutters that weren't designed for what Alabama weather actually does.</p>

<p>Meanwhile, the historic districts face the opposite problem. Five Points and Twickenham homes were built in the 1920s-40s, long before modern understanding of foundation drainage. Original systems were undersized — 4-inch gutters that can't handle what we get in a single March thunderstorm. Decades of settling have changed where water flows. And many of these beautiful homes are still running on gutter systems that should have been replaced twenty years ago.</p>

<p>After 20+ years working Huntsville rooftops, we've learned exactly what each neighborhood needs. The engineer in Research Park, the family in Jones Valley, the retiree on Monte Sano — they all face different drainage challenges. We know the difference, and we build systems accordingly.</p>
{% endset %}

{% set homeowner_guide %}
<p>If you own a home in Huntsville, here's your seasonal gutter checklist — the stuff that actually matters, not generic advice that could apply anywhere.</p>

<h4>Spring (March-May): Storm Season Prep</h4>
<p>This is the big one. Huntsville gets hit hard in spring — 4.7 inches of rain in March alone, followed by severe weather season in April. If you've lived here through April 27th (and every Huntsville resident knows that date), you understand what storms can do.</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Before the first major storm:</strong> Clear any winter debris from gutters. You don't want to enter tornado season with clogged drainage.</li>
<li><strong>After every significant storm:</strong> Walk your property. Look for debris in gutters, knocked-loose downspouts, or new pooling patterns near your foundation.</li>
<li><strong>Check your fascia boards:</strong> Winter moisture can cause hidden rot. Peeling paint along the roofline is a warning sign. Push on the wood with your thumb — if it gives at all, you've got problems.</li>
</ul>

<h4>Summer (June-August): The Afternoon Thunder Routine</h4>
<p>Huntsville's summer means daily afternoon thunderstorms — those 3 PM deluges that drop an inch of rain in 20 minutes. Your gutters get stress-tested constantly.</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Watch during a storm:</strong> If water pours over the front of your gutters, they're either clogged or undersized. Either way, that water is going somewhere bad.</li>
<li><strong>Check for heat damage:</strong> Vinyl components can warp in Alabama summer heat. Aluminum holds up better, but check for expansion gaps at seams. A little gap is normal — 3/16 inch or so. More than a quarter inch means something's wrong.</li>
<li><strong>Clear wasp nests:</strong> Downspouts are prime real estate for wasps. Check the outlet before they establish colonies. Trust us on this one.</li>
</ul>

<h4>Fall (September-December): The Pine Needle Problem</h4>
<p>If you're anywhere near Monte Sano, Big Cove, or the wooded areas of south Huntsville, fall means one thing: relentless pine needles.</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>September-October:</strong> Pine needle peak. Clean gutters or have them cleaned. Those needles slip through standard guards and create dense clogs that are a nightmare to clear later. By November they've turned into a solid mat that water can't penetrate.</li>
<li><strong>November:</strong> The oaks finally drop. Huntsville oaks hold their leaves late — don't do your "fall cleaning" too early. Mid-November is usually right.</li>
<li><strong>December:</strong> One final check before winter. Clear any remaining debris and make sure downspouts are flowing freely.</li>
</ul>

<h4>Winter (December-February): The Ice Surprise</h4>
<p>Alabama winters are mild — until they're not. We get ice events maybe once or twice a year, but when we do, unprepared gutters suffer.</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Before a freeze:</strong> Make sure gutters are draining. Standing water in gutters becomes ice, and ice is heavy — about 57 pounds per cubic foot. A 20-foot gutter section holding ice can pull right off the house.</li>
<li><strong>After an ice event:</strong> Check for damage before everything thaws. Ice dams can hide problems that won't show until the next rain.</li>
</ul>

<h4>What Makes Huntsville Different</h4>
<p>The combination of heavy rainfall (55+ inches annually), clay soil, spring severe weather, and rapid growth creates challenges you won't find in most cities. Gutters that work fine in Nashville or Atlanta struggle here. If you're having recurring problems, the system might not be wrong — it might just not be built for Huntsville.</p>
{% endset %}

{% set local_spotlight %}
<p>If you've driven through Five Points or Twickenham, you know why people love these neighborhoods. Tree-lined streets, front porches you actually use, neighbors who wave. Houses with character that new construction can't replicate.</p>

<p>We've worked on dozens of homes in Huntsville's historic districts, and here's what we've learned: these beautiful old houses hide their drainage problems well.</p>

<h4>The Hidden Damage Reality</h4>
<p>Many Five Points and Twickenham homes were built between 1900 and 1945. Their gutter systems — when original — were designed for a different era. Cast iron that's rusted through. Galvanized steel with corroded seams. Undersized 4-inch aluminum from a 1970s "upgrade" that was already inadequate when installed.</p>

<p>But the real issue is what's behind the gutters. Those decorative brackets and crown molding details? Perfect places for rot to develop unseen. We've pulled gutters off historic homes expecting a simple replacement and found fascia boards that crumbled at the touch — decades of overflow damage hidden behind what looked like solid paint.</p>

<h4>What We Typically See</h4>
<ul>
<li><strong>Undersized systems:</strong> Original gutters were designed for lighter rainfall expectations. They overflow during every significant storm.</li>
<li><strong>Failed seams:</strong> A hundred years of expansion and contraction takes a toll. Every joint is a potential leak point.</li>
<li><strong>Grading changes:</strong> Over decades, settling and landscaping modifications have altered where water flows. Drainage that worked in 1940 might be directing water toward the foundation now.</li>
<li><strong>Root interference:</strong> Those beautiful mature trees? Their root systems can affect underground drainage and create new pooling patterns.</li>
</ul>

<h4>How We Approach Historic Homes Differently</h4>
<p>Standard seamless gutters on a Twickenham Victorian would look wrong — and historic preservation matters to us too. Here's our approach:</p>

<p><strong>Half-round gutters</strong> maintain the period-appropriate aesthetic while providing modern capacity. Available in copper (which develops a beautiful patina) or painted aluminum that matches existing trim colors.</p>

<p><strong>Hidden hangers</strong> give modern support without visible fasteners that would look out of place on a 1920s Craftsman. We use heavy-duty hidden hangers rated for the weight loads these older systems need.</p>

<p><strong>Careful fascia work</strong> before any gutter installation. We're not going to mount new gutters on rotted wood and call it done. If the fascia needs repair, we do it right — matching existing profiles and materials when possible. We've spent entire mornings just prepping fascia on historic homes. It's worth it.</p>

<p>Your Five Points bungalow or Twickenham colonial has survived a century of Alabama weather. With proper drainage, it'll survive the next century too. And it'll still look like it belongs in the neighborhood — not like someone bolted modern equipment onto a historic home.</p>

<p>Questions about your specific historic property? We're happy to come take a look. No charge for the assessment — we just like an excuse to walk through those neighborhoods.</p>
